title: SecondLoopAaldenburg1
tags:
---

// plays when first talking to Aaldenburg, when getting the radio quest

There is not an idle moment for the woman in the scorched lab coat you see before you. 
She tinkers with this gizmo, she twiddles with another gadget, dancing through the lab in a practised smooth continuous motion. 
She is robotic, following a program of stimuli and response, a set of rules of how everything in the world should be touched, probed, read, and processed.
<< if $PsychMajor == true >>
    Her mannerisms are hard to parse without getting dizzy. 
    Your psychology know-how might not get you very far with her, though you can definitely tell she must have some sort of neurodevelopmental disorder.
<< elseif $ArtMajor == true >>
    The message she broadcasts is clear: She’s a serious woman of numbers and facts, and she has no care for whimsical nonsense. 
    It’s an ironic contrast with the way she hops between stations, almost like she is skipping. 
    Her half-black, half-red lipstick is quite pretty too.
<< elseif $TechMajor == true >>
	Before you’ve even talked to her, you already feel a rare kinship with her. 
    This is someone who sees the world exactly as it is, just like you: A huge complex machine, full of levers to pull, gears to oil, shiny lights to read and reactions to measure. 
<< endif >>
You stand still just outside the range of her work area, waiting for her to be done and to acknowledge your presence. 
After a few minutes, the flaws in this hypothesis become clear.

//If the player knows her name from Loop 1
-> “Excuse me, Dr. Aaldenburg?”
    There is no sign that she has heard you, for a full minute afterwards.
	Eventually however, she slowly comes to a stop, after finishing a few more tasks and clicking her pen for a last time.
	Aaldenburg: What is the matter?
	She raises her eyebrows in surprise once she finally takes a focused look at you.
	Aaldenburg: I have never seen you before. Are you from outside Solster?
-> “Excuse me, Miss?”
    She stops, abruptly, still holding a pen in one hand and a pipette in the other.
	???: Doctor. Not miss.
	Well, at least you got her to stop.
	You can finally take a good look at her. An id-card on her lapel identifies her as “Dr. Aaldenburg - Naturalist Physicist - Nauka Square”.
    << if $TechMajor == true >>
	    Darn. You were hoping for another fellow engineer.
    << endif >>
	She raises her eyebrows in surprise now that she takes a good look at you as well.
    Aaldenburg: I have never seen you before. Are you from outside Solster?
-> “Could you stop for a second?”
    Either she can’t, or she won’t. She continues her endless tinkering with no reaction to your presence. 
    Just when you are about to talk again, she finally comes to a stop, after finishing a few more tasks and clicking her pen for the last time.
    ???: What is the matter?
    You can finally take a good look at her. An id-card on her lapel identifies her as “Dr. Aaldenburg - Naturalist Physicist - Nauka Square”.
    << if $TechMajor == true >>
	    Darn. You were hoping for another fellow engineer.
    << endif >>
    She raises her eyebrows in surprise now that she takes a good look at you as well.
    Aaldenburg: I have never seen you before. Are you from outside Solster?

-> “I’m Sasha. I was born here, but I went to college away from here.”
    Aaldenburg: I suspected so. Your accent says Solster, your outfit says student. Graduate now I take it?
	You can’t tell if that outfit comment was meant to be a dig at you or not. Having talked to Wisteria before might have predisposed you to see insults where none were intended.
	Aaldenburg: Graduates of higher education are an uncommon, but welcome sight here in Solster. What did you major in?
-> “No, I’m from here. My name is Sasha.”
    Aaldenburg: I can tell from your accent, yes. Yet I have not seen you or heard from you before, and you seem hard to miss in a town this size.
	Is she saying you look strange? You can’t tell if that comment was meant to be a dig at you or not. 
    Having talked to Wisteria before might have predisposed you to see insults where none were intended.
    Aaldenburg: Are you a college student by chance?
        -> “I was. I graduated.”
		She seems pleased with herself for having figured that out.
		Aaldenburg: The hypothesis is proved correct. You do still have that rebellious college student aesthetic. It must have been a recent graduation. 
        << if $ArtMajor == true >>
		    Curious that she is familiar with the ‘college student’ aesthetic. It makes you wonder what she was like back in her own college days. You have a feeling she was quite different to how she is now…
        << endif >>
		Aaldenburg: Graduates of higher education are an uncommon, but welcome sight here in Solster. What did you major in?
	-> “How did you guess that?”
		Aaldenburg gives a light chuckle, or something close to it. Nothing about her face smiles at any moment of it.
		Aaldenburg: I did not guess it, I hypothesised it based on the information I could gather.
		Aaldenburg: You look college aged, and your outfit has some of that rebellious aesthetic that students love. It would explain why I had never seen you before, too.
        << if $ArtMajor == true >>
		    Curious that she is familiar with the ‘college student’ aesthetic. It makes you wonder what she was like back in her own college days. You have a feeling she was quite different to how she is now…
        << endif >>
		Aaldenburg: Since you are back at Solster, I can further hypothesise that you are either visiting family, or graduated. If I were to make an actual ‘guess’, I would say graduated.
		Aaldenburg: This town is not a place one comes back to for the holidays. I could not imagine all that travel to come here, for what?
		Her tone is dismissive, seemingly of the idea that anyone would enjoy coming to Solster at all. You wonder if she’s ever met Fabian.
		Aaldenburg: Graduates of higher education are an uncommon, but welcome sight here in Solster. What did you major in?
-> “I’m Sasha. I’m pretty much an outsider here.”
    Aaldenburg: Pretty much? That is curiously non-committal language to use to describe your status. 
	She looks at you, up and down. It reminds you of how Wisteria looked at you too, like she is analysing you through a microscope.
	Aaldenburg: Your accent sounds quite Solsterian for an outsider too. Are you a college student by chance?
        -> “I was. I graduated.”
		    She seems pleased with herself for having figured that out.
		    Aaldenburg: The hypothesis is proved correct. You do still have that rebellious college student aesthetic. It must have been a recent graduation. 
		    Curious that she is familiar with the ‘college student’ aesthetic. It makes you wonder what she was like back in her own college days. You have a feeling she was quite different to how she is now…
		    Aaldenburg: Graduates of higher education are an uncommon, but welcome sight here in Solster. What did you major in?
	-> “How did you guess that?”
		    Aaldenburg gives a light chuckle, or something close to it. Nothing about her face smiles at any moment of it.
		    Aaldenburg: I did not guess it, I hypothesised it based on the information I could gather.
		    Aaldenburg: You look college aged, and your outfit has some of that rebellious aesthetic that students love. It would explain why I had never seen you before, too.
		    Curious that she is familiar with the ‘college student’ aesthetic. It makes you wonder what she was like back in her own college days. You have a feeling she was quite different to how she is now…
