<< if $PsychMajor == true >>
	-> Psychology.
	    Aaldenburg: A people-person I see. I’m sure Dr. Wisteria will be delighted.
	    She says this in a tone that lacks even the barest note of enthusiasm. Having met Dr. Wisteria yourself, you fully understand why.
	    You feel the instinct to tell her that she was absolutely not delighted to meet you, but Aaldenburg seems like the type of person who doesn’t like being told things they already know.
<< elseif $ArtMajor == true >>
	-> I went to art school.
	    A brief grimace washes over her face. She is equal parts incredulous and disappointed. As an art major, it's a reaction you are used to.
	    Aaldenburg: Really? I took you for more of an intellectual sort. 
        Aaldenburg: Well, at least you're bringing something new to the town. Artistic pursuits are still more stimulating than just staring at the lake, how most people spend their free time here.
        Not a lot of people in Solster have hobbies. The Solsterian work ethic is ruthless. 
        You’d have expected that Aaldenburg would be behind that, seeing how dedicated she seems to her work. Maybe passion is an important factor for her.
<< elseif $TechMajor == true >>
    -> Engineering.
	    Her eyes barely widen, and her mouth open just ever so slightly. She fails to repress a momentary expression of glee over her face, before returning almost mechanically to her regular robotic look.
	    Aaldenburg: It is always pleasant to meet another fellow professional in the field of the practical sciences.
	    Aaldenburg: Perhaps you are still too green to call you  a professional, but I am sure we will get along well.
	    Aaldenburg: My expertise is in Chemistry, but I dabble in various fields. I have little other choice, given the staffing of my laboratory.
	    With that she means that she has nobody else to work with, from the looks of it. There is another lab coat hanging at the entrance, though. 
	    Does she have an assistant, or is it just a spare?
<< endif >>
A nearby machine suddenly emits a beep. Aaldenburg turns her attention to it, turning a dial down and writing a reading from its display on her notebook.
Aaldenburg: I would not mind chatting with you further on another occasion, but I am rather busy at this time, as you can see. Was there a reason you came to see me?

-> “I just traveled back in time a full day after seeing the void destroy Solster.”
        Aaldenburg: No, you did not. Anything else?
	    If she were less busy, she might have been willing to indulge you, as an exercise in science fantasy, but she does not have the time to hear you out it seems.
	    Your radio issues might get her attention better. Maybe you can circle back onto the whole ‘time travel’ issue once you have dealt with that.
        -> “My radio is acting very strange. I thought maybe someone here at Nauka Square would now what is happening to it”
            Aaldenburg: You have a radio? They are quite rare here in Solster. 
	        Aaldenburg: I am not a repairwoman, but you have made me curious now. Show me.
	        You take out the radio from your pocket. Compared to earlier, it has gone mostly silent, but when you put your ear close, you can still hear some static brewing. 
        -> “Wisteria sent me here to get my radio fixed.”
            Aaldenburg gives you a look as if she had bitten an onion the moment you mention Wisteria.
	        Aaldenburg: Oh, of course she is behind this. She does not miss an opportunity to try to disrupt my work on the day of the parade.
	        Aaldenburg: Fine. Show me that radio.
	        You take out the radio from your pocket. Compared to earlier, it has gone mostly silent, but when you put your ear close, you can still hear some static brewing. 
//Circles back to other two options
-> “My radio is acting very strange. I thought maybe someone here at Nauka Square would now what is happening to it”
    Aaldenburg: You have a radio? They are quite rare here in Solster. 
	Aaldenburg: I am not a repairwoman, but you have made me curious now. Show me.
	You take out the radio from your pocket. Compared to earlier, it has gone mostly silent, but when you put your ear close, you can still hear some static brewing. 
-> “Wisteria sent me here to get my radio fixed.”
    Aaldenburg gives you a look as if she had bitten an onion the moment you mention Wisteria.
	Aaldenburg: Oh, of course she is behind this. She does not miss an opportunity to try to disrupt my work on the day of the parade.
	Aaldenburg: Fine. Show me that radio.
	You take out the radio from your pocket. Compared to earlier, it has gone mostly silent, but when you put your ear close, you can still hear some static brewing. 

Aaldenburg: Ah, an Adalard 22. It is a good model, one of the better domestic radios one can find in the market. You have a good eye.
<< if $TechMajor == true >>
    She has a good eye for this as well, for someone whose speciality lies not in machines. Judging by all the contraptions in this lab, she must have a good amount of knowledge of electronics as well.
<< endif >>
Aaldenburg places the radio on her work desk and begins her examination. She looks at it from every angle, touching every button and turning every dial.
In the meantime, you explain to her what you experienced, the sudden static and interference, and the voice mixed within.
Eventually, she takes a screwdriver and opens it up, looking carefully at the mechanisms within without touching them.
After a few minutes, she reassembles your radio and hands it back. Seems she put it back together perfectly, to your relief.
Aaldenburg: There do not appear to be any defects or malfunctions on the radio itself. Thus, there is nothing to fix here.
Aaldenburg: But… I admit I am curious about that interference you encountered. 
Aaldenburg: As I said, there are very few radios in Solster outside of this laboratory. You might have stumbled upon a novel phenomenon that has not been recorded before. This warrants further exploration.
Suddenly, she walks away deeper in her lab. She opens a cabinet and starts digging around for something inside, amongst a mess of wires and machine parts.
Before you can ask for an explanation, she comes back just as quick and puts a small device into your hand. It has a screen, antenna, and some simple buttons and dials.
Aaldenburg: You are going to do some field work. Take this GPS, and go into the town to find some places where the radio interference is present. Understood?

-> “How does this ‘GPS’ work? I have never heard of it.”
    Aaldenburg: It is a fairly new invention, with limited application. I got one for testing from my old university, as Solster is perfect grounds for a trial.
	Aaldenburg: The GPS sends and receives constant signals to and from a receptor here at Nauka Square. 
    Aaldenburg: By measuring the time between signals and their direction, the device can accurately display your exact coordinates within the town as a vector from the receptor.
    Aaldenburg: It will let you note down your position in numbers, much more precise than using a map.
    -> “What do I have to do exactly?”
        Aaldenburg moves over to a map of Solster hanging on a nearby wall. The void lake is on the center, with the whole town built around it.
	    Aaldenburg: We are going to triangulate the source of the radio interference, by finding several locations around Solster where your radio experiences said interference.
	    Aaldenburg: We already know the Star Hospital is one such location. If you can find three more places where your radio reacts to the interference, we should be able to locate where it is coming from.
	    Aaldenburg: Be precise. If your radio starts making that static noise, then move around your current area until you locate the exact position where the static is the strongest.
	    Aaldenburg: Do you understand your task now?
-> “What do I have to do exactly?”
    Aaldenburg moves over to a map of Solster hanging on a nearby wall. The void lake is on the center, with the whole town built around it.
	Aaldenburg: We are going to triangulate the source of the radio interference, by finding several locations around Solster where your radio experiences said interference.
	Aaldenburg: We already know the Star Hospital is one such location. If you can find three more places where your radio reacts to the interference, we should be able to locate where it is coming from.
	Aaldenburg: Be precise. If your radio starts making that static noise, then move around your current area until you locate the exact position where the static is the strongest.
	Aaldenburg: Do you understand your task now?

//Unlocks after “what do I do exactly?”
-> “Any ideas of where I might find this static?”
    Aaldenburg does not answer right away. She ponders, looking around her lab while she thinks.
	Aaldenburg: The first time the static occurred while you were at the Hospital, right?
	Aaldenburg: The hospital does have some machines in it that could have played a factor, but we can’t state that for certain.
	Aaldenburg: My advice would be to stay observant of what is around you once you do find a place where the static is present. Perhaps in similar places, the static will be present too.
	Aaldenburg: As to how you should find your first place with static, that is merely a matter of legwork. 
    Aaldenburg: You could try to investigate areas of Solster that you have not gone to yet or have transited less since you came back. That would be a good course of action. 
        -> “You’re not coming with me?”
            Aaldenburg: Did you forget already when, approximately seven minutes ago, I said I was currently busy?
	        Aaldenburg: I have already given you more of my time than I do most visitors. Now it is your turn to do your part.
	        Aaldenburg: Plus, field work is good for you. It is good practice, builds experience. I imagine they did not send you outside in your study very often, no?
            << if $PsychMajor == true >>
                You did do some practical assignments, but you suspect her standards for what “practical” means are stricter than those of your teachers.
	            It did not involve anywhere as much walking as she probably believes is necessary.
            << elseif $ArtMajor == true >>
                You’ve been to places for your study, though mainly museums and parks for live drawing. Not so much waddling in mud or climbing up trees to find a radio signal.
            << elseif $TechMajor == true >>
                They did not, no. Your practical assignments primarily took place in a workshop, one not too different from Nauka Square.
            << endif >>
-> “You’re not coming with me?”
    Aaldenburg: Did you forget already when, approximately seven minutes ago, I said I was currently busy?
	Aaldenburg: I have already given you more of my time than I do most visitors. Now it is your turn to do your part.
	Aaldenburg: Plus, field work is good for you. It is good practice, builds experience. I imagine they did not send you outside in your study very often, no?
