	// Loops back, with this option removed
        -> “Any ideas of where I might find this static?”
            Aaldenburg does not answer right away. She ponders, looking around her lab while she thinks.
	        Aaldenburg: The first time the static occurred while you were at the Hospital, right?
	        Aaldenburg: The hospital does have some machines in it that could have played a factor, but we can’t state that for certain.
	        Aaldenburg: My advice would be to stay observant of what is around you once you do find a place where the static is present. Perhaps in similar places, the static will be present too.
	        Aaldenburg: As to how you should find your first place with static, that is merely a matter of legwork. 
            Aaldenburg: You could try to investigate areas of Solster that you have not gone to yet or have transited less since you came back. That would be a good course of action. 

//Unlocks when you have selected at least the “What do I do exactly” option.
-> “Okay, I think I am ready to go out then.”
	Aaldenburg: Good. You can come back once you have found three static locations, other than the hospital.
	Aaldenburg: Be careful with the GPS. If you break it, you will pay for it.
	Her frown seems to say that you’ll pay for it in more than just money.
	Aaldenburg goes back to her workbench and resumes her tinkering, right back where she left off. 
	You take a look at the device in your hand. The screen reads [2.38161, 5.19372]. 
    << if $TechMajor == true >>
        The display is likely the x and y factors of a distance vector between the device and one of the antennas you saw on the roof of the building.
	    Is it connecting to that big, gold antenna you saw? This could be something to ask about later.
    << else>> 
	    You’re glad that you don’t need to understand how this works.
	<< endif >>
	
	For now, it is time to leave this lab, and go out into Solster.


